From: timstarr@netcom.com (Tim Starr)
Date: Sun, 4 Dec 1994 21:50:06 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-143] Machinery of Friedman

>From: John K Clark <johnkc@well.sf.ca.us>
>Date: Sat, 3 Dec 1994 13:39:42 -0800
>Subject: [#94-12-105] Say it's not so !
>
>"The Machinery of Freedom" out of print, APPALLING !!! Page for
>page I've never seen a book that had as many good provocative 
>ideas. I've read it 3 times and still think I can get more out
>of it. Tim Starr says that Friedman didn't handle the "knowledge
>problem" well , could you elaborate, are you referring to the
>"efficient market theory"? Also I've been thinking of ordering "
>The Enterprise of Law" but it's rather expensive, is it worth it?

By "the knowledge problem," I refer to the difficulty of knowing
what another's preferences are or would be.  David seems to say
that the fact that we can give each other presents which are liked
by the getter means that there isn't any such problem.  This indicates
a failure to appreciate the problem.

First of all, the problem isn't absolute, it's comparative.  In other
words, the problem isn't whether we can know others' preferences, but
whether we can know them as well as they can.

Secondly, when it comes to State action, the analogy to gift-giving
breaks down.  The State doesn't buy gifts with its own hard-earned
money to give to its citizens.  The State steals money from its
citizens and then buys what it can get away with, some of which is
given to the citizens.

