From: timstarr@netcom.com (Tim Starr)
Date: Sun, 4 Dec 1994 22:16:35 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-144] BASICS: Reply to Fred (was: Machineries, etc.)

>From: fhapgood@world.std.com (Fred Hapgood)
>Date: Sun, 4 Dec 1994 18:34:24 -0500
>Subject: [#94-12-134] Machineries, etc.
>
>>...What??  Move?  Why would I want to move?  Why wouldn't I just pay
>>guys to go around and take kids away from parents who are giving
>>them the wrong kind of attention?  
>
>Starr repostes:
>
>      It's cheaper.
>
>Huh?...

Your money will go farther in neighborhoods with like-minded people,
because there will tend to be less of the crime.  You get more bang
for the buck.  If your goal is to make things safe for yourself &
your neighbors, it'll be cheaper to have neighbors that won't endanger
your safety.

If your goal is to eradicate all child abuse from the entire world,
you'll soon go broke, as will the rest of your organization.  If your
goal is to eliminate as much of it as possible given the amount of
money your organization can raise sustainably, then it will have a lot
of angry parents to fight off - possibly armed ones with lots of allies.
And a lot of angry adults bent on revenge for being kidnapped by your
organization as children.  The more violent the tactics of your group,
the more violent the tactics of those who retaliate against it.  The
odds that your organization will survive all the violent retaliation
it provokes will be small.

>Nick Szabo says:
>>
>>I presume that enforcement of a contract is not considered
>>initiation of force...
>
>Starr pounces:
>
>      Bingo!
>
>...While it might be true in an
>abstract and technical sense that enforcement of a contract is
>not coercion, in real life, when at least some of the disputes
>you get into are going to be decided against you, over your
>furious objections, the distinction soon evaporates.

The distinction remains that if you refuse to comply with enforcement
of a contract against the decision of an arbitrator in what appears to
be a fair hearing, you're likely to have few allies/sympathizers.  The
enforcers, on the other hand, are likely to have many.

However, since the enforcers won't have the State to externalize the
costs of enforcement, they'll be more likely to try to gain your
cooperation.  And since the judges future livelihood will depend upon
mutual acceptance by both parties to future disputes, they'll be more
likely to try to make their decisions acceptable to both parties.

