John K Clark wrote:
> 	     
> Uploads would probably eventually develop advanced VR but they
> wouldn't  need to if they were content with just interfacing
> with the real world. VR is not an enabling technology, it would
> be a luxury not a necessity for an upload.

Uploads would need a VR if they were operating in a faster time 
frame than the real world.  They would also need computers to get 
anything done in their VR.  These computers would be more useless 
the faster the human brains operated.  Since, in this hypothetical 
situation, computers would probably be the primary tool for technological 
development, the uploads would not be developing much of anything.
	      
> Even today some electronic switches work 100
> million times faster than neural synapses and nanoelectronic
> switches would be far faster...
> ...now all I have to do is crank up
> the "clock speed" a few billion times.
 
You would not be replacing the neuron with an artificial one, but with an 
analogous program.  So the limit in speed is not in the switching speed 
of the electric switch, but in the speed of the program which would 
employ many switches.  The computer used to run the simulation of the 
brain puts a limit on the speed increase that can be achieved.  If humans 
are uploaded as soon as a computer is designed which is complex enough to 
hold the simulation (a big if), the simulation _could not be_ sped up no 
matter how much you rationalized that it should be.  I thought this was 
obvious.

> 			>There are natural limits to energy dissipation 
> 			
> Not for computation. Landauer, Bennett and Merkle have shown
> that with reversible computing the amount of energy needed to
> make a calculation can be arbitrarily small.

Fine.  I'm still talking about the first day after the 'singularity,' and 
I was referring more to waste energy produced in physical manipulation of 
our solar system and not just to computation.

Godshatter wrote:

> The process could start with gradual replacement of traditional parts 
> of the body with better ones - including a gradual replacement...

Yes.  GRADUAL.  That is my point.  It will not happen suddenly, but will 
be gradual.

