From: Reilly Jones <70544.1227@compuserve.com>
Date: 05 Dec 94 03:20:02 EST
Subject: [#94-12-150] MEDIA: Radio show impressions

Vince Kerchner wrote 12/4/94: <I am a little concerned about what kind of first
impression Extropianism makes on the average person.  This morning's
interview is a good example.  In order, the topics raised seemed to be:

  1. Leather.
  2. Caller-ID.
  3. Head-freezing.>

What negative consequences do you see from any impression whatsoever that
Extropianism has on the "average" person?  What will they do with their
impressions?  Why the concern for this particular media segment, if serious
purveyors of high culture or in your words "those with the capacity and
temperment to understand [the basic tenets]" are who should be interested in
Extropianism?

<I do not think of Extropianism as primarily a political vector.  In order, I
see extropianism as being about:

  1. Philosophy.
  2. Technology.
  3. Economics.

In particular, I am concerned about the augmentation of radical political and
"sensational" issues at the expense of thoughtful philosophy.  I am an advocate
of the codification of idealistic viewpoints, and, specifically, of the
Extropian Principles, which I happen to like, agree with, and feel are an often
underrated achievement.  However, I have noticed that, during the past 2 years,
I seem to be in an ever decreasing minority concerning this issue.>

Radical political and sensational issues are much easier, and more attention
grabbing than thoughtful philosophy, especially before being implemented on any
decent-sized scale to see what their consequences are.  Many people by
temperament think action, any action, is preferable to words.  Technology, being
action, is a major focus point.  Philosophy as a historical endeavor is barely
entering puberty, and economics as a science isn't out of diapers yet.
Beginnings are difficult, overextension is always a danger, so is overcaution.
I do not underrate the Principles, they are sketches, hints of something that
might work better than all other competing worldviews.  Don't worry about being
in a decreasing minority, the search for truth is thankfully not a democratic
process, but a selectional system.

On a cross-thread issue, you are being such a wet blanket about the singularity,
nanotech, rapture of the future, etc., even to the point of invoking the dread
term "mysticism."  <g>  An observation that Tacitus made in _Agricola_ might
help to explain the fervor in all this:  "Everything that is unknown is taken to
be grand."  If everything becomes unknown, everything becomes grand.

